<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Karkiya dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1370 CE – 1592 CE (approx.) · capital Lahijan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Shia dynasty of eastern Gilan, centred on Lahijan, that sheltered the boy who became Shah Ismail I, founder of the Safavid state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-ali-kiya">Sayyid Ali Kiya</a>, a Zaydi sayyid supported by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marashi-dynasty">Mar'ashi dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> (Biyapish) around 1370. His descendants, the Kar-Kiya, ruled this part of Gilan east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sefidrud">Sefidrud</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Karkiya combined religious authority as sayyids with princely rule over a wet, forested land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and fishing. They long followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaydism">Zaydi Shia Islam</a>, later adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a>, and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ishaqid-dynasty">Ishaqid dynasty</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mirza-karkiya">Ali Mirza Karkiya</a> gave refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a> from 1494 to 1499 to the young Ismail, heir of the Safavid order, who left from there to found the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khan-ahmad-khan">Khan Ahmad Khan</a>, the last ruler, quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> in 1592; Khan Ahmad fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karkiya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Karkiya dynasty" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_northern_Iran_in_1425.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1370 CE – 1592 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lahijan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sayyid Ali Kiya, Sultan Ali Mirza, Khan Ahmad Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zaydi, later Twelver Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gilaki, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kar-Kiya, Kia'i dynasty, Kingdom of Biyapish</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1481&amp;focus=karkiya-dynasty" aria-label="Karkiya dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5932 838 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karkiya dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6252 1094L6281 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6281 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6252 1094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6252 1094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lahijan. <a href="/atlas?year=1481&amp;focus=karkiya-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

